fixed parameters:\nL316: \nL317:   * •\nL318: \nL319: Persona generator: Expert\nL320: \nL321:   * •\nL322: \nL323: Discussion paradigm: Memory\nL324: \nL325:   * •\nL326: \nL327: Decision protocol: Average of Simple Voting, Ranked Voting, Cumulative Voting and Approval Voting\nL328: Each experiment is repeated three times, and the average performance and standard deviation across the runs are reported.\nL329: ### E.2 Discussion Experiments\nL330: \nL331: We use meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-70B-Instruct as a model for all agents. We further report the parameters that are set fixed for this experiment:\nL332: \nL333:   * •\nL334: \nL335: Persona generator: Expert\nL336: \nL337:   * •\nL338: \nL339: Response generator: Simple\nL340: \nL341:   * •\nL342: \nL343: Decision protocol: Majority Consensus\nL344: \nL345: To ensure the reliability of our findings, we follow the prior work of cite160†Wang et al. (2024a) and conduct each experiment five times, reporting the average performance and standard deviation across the runs.\nL346: ### E.3 Decision Experiments\nL347: \nL348: The setup and parameters for this experiment are described in cite65†Kaesberg et al. (2025) . They use meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct as a model for all agents with the following fixed parameters:\nL349: \nL350:   * •\nL351: \nL352: Persona generator: Expert\nL353: \nL354:   * •\nL355: \nL356: Discussion paradigm: Memory\nL357: \nL358:   * •\nL359: \nL360: Response Generator: Simple\nL361: \nL362: Each experiment is repeated three times, and the average performance and standard deviation across the runs are reported.\nL363: ## Appendix F Evaluation Pipeline\nL364: \nL365: cite98†Figures 10 , cite99†7 , cite101†8 and cite100†9 show example charts generated by the MALLM evaluation pipeline. They are presented to demonstrate the pipeline’s automated analysis and visualization capabilities. These specific examples are generated from experiments on the StrategyQA dataset using the Llama 3 8B model, with error bars representing the standard deviation across three runs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMALLM: Multi-Agent Large Language Models Framework (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.11656v1)\nciteturn3187view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3184view2\",\"pattern\":\"Results\"}); Total lines: 539\nL155: Automatic charts. MALLM can visualize the evaluation results of MAD configurations. For this, researchers can pass a single file or a directory with multiple evaluation results directly to an automatic chart generator. Examples of the generated charts by the evaluation pipeline can be seen in cite98†Figures 10 , cite99†7 , cite100†9 and cite101†8 of cite29†Appendix F .\nL410: Example MALLM batch configuration file for running an experiment with fixed response generator, persona generator, and discussion paradigm, but varying decision protocols. The \"repeats\" field defines how many times each run is repeated, which is later relevant for evaluating for robustness by the standard deviation between experiment runs. The \"common\" field describes parameters that are considered for all runs. The \"runs\" field defines the parameters unique for each individual run.\nL411: \nL412: ⬇\nL413: \nL414: 1 {\nL415: 2 \"repeats\": 3,\nL416: \nL417: 3 \"name\": \"<DATASET NAME>\",\nL418: \nL419: 4 \"common\": {\nL420: \nL421: 5 \"task_instruction_prompt_template\": \"<DATASET NAME>\",\nL422: \nL423: 6 \"endpoint_url\": \"<LLM API HOSTNAME>\",\nL424: \nL425: 7 \"api_key\": \"<LLM API KEY>\",\nL426: \nL427: 8 \"model_name\": \"<MODEL NAME>\",\nL428: \nL429: 9 \"input_json_file_path\": \"data/datasets/<DATASET NAME>.json\",\nL430: \nL431: 10 \"concurrent_api_requests\": 200,\nL432: \nL433: 11 \"num_samples\": \"<NUMBER OF SAMPLES>\",\nL434: \nL435: 12 \"max_turns\": 5,\nL436: \nL437: 13 \"response_generator\":\"simple\"\nL438: \nL439: 14 },\nL440: \nL441: 15 \"runs\": [\nL442: \nL443: 16 {\nL444: \nL445: 17 \"output_json_file_path\": \"results/baseline-cot.json\",\nL446: 18 \"use_baseline\": true\nL447: \nL448: 19 },\nL449: \nL450: 20 {\nL451: \nL452: 21 \"output_json_file_path\": \"results/baseline.json\",\nL453: \nL454: 22 \"use_baseline\": true,\nL455: \nL456: 23 \"use_chain_of_thought\": false\nL457: \nL458: 24 },\nL459: \nL460: 25 {\nL461: \nL462: 26 \"output_json_file_path\": \"results/approval.json\",\nL463: \nL464: 27 \"decision_protocol\": \"approval_voting\"\nL465: \nL466: 28 },\nL467: \nL468: 29 {\nL469: \nL470: 30 \"output_json_file_path\": \"results/cumulative.json\",\nL471: \nL472: 31 \"decision_protocol\": \"cumulative_voting\"\nL473: \nL474: 32 },\nL475: \nL476: 33 {\nL477: \nL478: 34 \"output_json_file_path\": \"results/majority_consensus.json\",\nL479: \nL480: 35 \"decision_protocol\": \"majority_consensus\"\nL481: \nL482: 36 },\nL483: \nL484: 37 {\nL485: 38 \"output_json_file_path\": \"results/supermajority_consensus.json\",\nL486: \nL487: 39 \"decision_protocol\": \"supermajority_consensus\"\nL488: \nL489: 40 },\nL490: \nL491: 41 {\nL492: \nL493: 42 \"output_json_file_path\": \"results/unanimity_consensus.json\",\nL494: \nL495: 43 \"decision_protocol\": \"unanimity_consensus\"\nL496: \nL497: 44 },\nL498: \nL499: 45 {\nL500: \nL501: 46 \"output_json_file_path\": \"results/voting.json\",\nL502: \nL503: 47 \"decision_protocol\": \"simple_voting\"\nL504: \nL505: 48 },\nL506: \nL507: 49 {\nL508: \nL509: 50 \"output_json_file_path\": \"results/ranked.json\",\nL510: \nL511: 51 \"decision_protocol\": \"ranked_voting\"\nL512: \nL513: 52 }\nL514: \nL515: 53 ]\nL516: \nL517: 54 }\nL518: CiteAssist\nL519: Citation Sheet\nL520: Generated with cite168†citeassist.uni-goettingen.de†citeassist.uni-goettingen.de L521: \nL522: Generated August 24, 2026\nL523: \nL524: Experimental support, please cite169†view the build logs for errors. Generated by cite170†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov .\nL525: ## Instructions for reporting errors\nL526: \nL527: We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below:\nL528: \nL529:   * Click the \"Report Issue\" () button, located in the page header.\nL530: \nL531: Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report.\nL532: Our team has already identified cite171†the following issues†github.com . We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us improve the HTML versions for all readers, because disability should not be a barrier to accessing research. Thank you for your continued support in championing open access for all.\nL533: Have a free development cycle? Help support accessibility at arXiv! Our collaborators at LaTeXML maintain a cite172†list of packages that need conversion†github.com , and welcome cite173†developer contributions†github.com .\nL534: \nL535: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite174†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL536: cite175†About†info.arxiv.org · cite176†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite177†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite178†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite179†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite180†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite181†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite182†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L537: \nL538: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMALLM: Multi-Agent Large Language Models Framework (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.11656v1)\nciteturn3187view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3184view2\",\"pattern\":\"4 \"}); Total lines: 539\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:   4. cite8†3 MALLM Framework L19:     1. cite9†3.1 Agents L20:       1. cite10†Persona Generator. L21:       2. cite11†Response Generator. L22:     2. cite12†3.2 Discussion Paradigm L23:     3. cite13†3.3 Decision Protocol L24:     4. cite14†3.4 Evaluation L25:   5. cite15†4 Application L26:     1. cite16†4.1 Use Cases L27:     2. cite17†4.2 Example Experiments with MALLM L28:   6. cite18†5 Epilogue L29:   7. cite19†6 Acknowledgements L30:   8. cite20†References L31:   9. cite21†A MALLM Workflow L32:   10. cite22†B System Demonstration L148: ### 3.4 Evaluation\nL149: \nL150: The MALLM framework includes a pipeline for evaluating MAD configurations, producing statistics and charts for a dataset and its metrics.\nL151: Datasets. The pipeline provides integrated loaders for reasoning tasks (e.g., WinoGrande (cite86†Sakaguchi et al., 2019 ), StrategyQA (cite87†Geva et al., 2021 )) and knowledge tasks (e.g., GPQA (cite88†Rein et al., 2023 ), MMLU-Pro (cite89†Wang et al., 2024c )) as well as core tasks of text generation (cite90†Becker et al., 2024 ), such as paraphrasing (cite91†Kovatchev et al., 2018 ) or summarization (cite92†Narayan et al., 2018 ). It further supports any textual Huggingface dataset for problem-solving.\nL152: Researchers can also add their own dataset via subclassing. All datasets are converted into a unified format for processing.\nL153: Metrics. We include question-answering and free-text evaluations. For question-answering, we compute accuracy by comparing selected response letters against reference solutions via regex. For free-text tasks, we include BERTScore (cite93†Zhang et al., 2020 ) and textual overlap measures (BLEU (cite94†Papineni et al., 2002 ), ROUGE-1/2/3/L (cite95†Lin, 2004 ), and METEOR (cite96†Banerjee and Lavie, 2005 )).\nL154: Statistical variance. We find that several studies on MAD do not account for statistical variance (cite66†Wu et al., 2023 ; cite97†Talebirad and Nadiri, 2023 ), yet it can have marked impacts on MAD (cite74†Smit et al., 2024 ). MALLM enables repeated experiments and calculates standard deviations between them, thereby quantifying statistical robustness.\nL155: Automatic charts. MALLM can visualize the evaluation results of MAD configurations. For this, researchers can pass a single file or a directory with multiple evaluation results directly to an automatic chart generator. Examples of the generated charts by the evaluation pipeline can be seen in cite98†Figures 10 , cite99†7 , cite100†9 and cite101†8 of cite29†Appendix F .\nL156: ## 4 Application\nL157: We explain the workflow for using MALLM to evaluate a specific MAD configuration. First, we can load any text-based Huggingface dataset for problem-solving via our dataset loader (e.g., MMLU-Pro (cite89†Wang et al., 2024c )). Second, MALLM comes with a configuration file (cf.\nL158: cite42†Appendix H ) that the user can change to adjust parameters and the components for the desired experiment (e.g., using Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct as a model and changing the configuration to four debating agents, Relay discussion paradigm, and Unanimity Voting decision protocol). Third, we can run the experiment specified by the configuration file. MALLM keeps extensive logs of all debates.\nL159: Lastly, we can directly feed the logs into our evaluation pipeline, computing metrics (e.g., accuracy) and leveraging automatic chart generation (cf. cite98†Figures 10 , cite99†7 , cite101†8 and cite100†9 in cite29†Appendix F ).\nL160: Each component (agent, discussion, decision) comes with abstract base classes to describe the pipeline of MAD. If necessary, a custom component could be provided by inheriting features from a component’s abstract base class. For example, Sketch-of-Thought (cite102†Aytes et al., 2025 ) just got published, a variant of Chain-of-Thought (cite103†Wei et al., 2023 ) that restructures how models express intermediate steps of their reasoning.\nL161: To integrate with MALLM, we would create a custom class SoTResponseGenerator inheriting from the abstract base class ResponseGenerator. Then, MAD can be defined by a configuration file as usual. cite104†Figure 5 in cite21†Appendix A provides an overview of the workflow for conducting and evaluating MAD with MALLM.\nL162: ### 4.1 Use Cases\nL163: \nL164: The following examples illustrate possible research directions that can be realized using MALLM.\nL165: \nL166: Agents on paradigms. The number of agents for the MAD is modifiable. Researchers can test the impact of this on the various discussion paradigms. For example, a study could compare two, three, four, or five agents on the memory paradigm with the relay paradigm. As these paradigms differ in their information flow, it would be interesting to see how this impacts task performance.\nL167: Testing new task. JailbreakBench (cite105†Chao et al., 2024 ) measures the safety of LLMs against jailbreaks. One avenue could be the comparison of multi-agent safety with a single-agent setup. For this, we can use MALLM, which is plug-and-play with a templated configuration set.\nL168: Finetuned agents. MALLM works with any proprietary and open LLM, which means that we can also provide our own model for the agents. A promising direction is to fine-tune an agent to enhance its argumentation skills, thereby improving task performance on reasoning tasks such as StrategyQA (cite87†Geva et al., 2021 ).\nL169: Moderated paradigm. A dynamic moderator can be implemented by subclassing the abstract base class DiscussionParadigm. We can define logic for an LLM-based moderator agent to adjust speaking order based on previous agent contributions. This enables an investigation into the effects of adaptive moderation.\nL170: ### 4.2 Example Experiments with MALLM\nL171: \nL172: MALLM can be applied to various use cases. To demonstrate the opportunities for experimental setups, we provide some example investigations. Supplementary information, such as model parameters, can be found in cite25†Appendix E .\nL174: The Reasoning response generator decreases performance by 6.4% points, likely because it imposes a strict response structure. Strictly structured responses can degrade the task performance of MAD, a characteristic that was previously noted in single-agent setups (cite107†Tam et al., 2024 ). To summarize, invoking strict response patterns from agents can harm task performance, while prompting agents to think critically can boost it.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEmoBench-Reddit: A Hierarchical Benchmark for Evaluating the Emotional Intelligence of Multimodal Large Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.11101v1)\nciteturn3187view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3184view1\",\"pattern\":\"6.2\"}); Total lines: 356\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:     1. cite8†2.1 MLLM Evaluation Benchmarks L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Exploring and Evaluating Emotional Intelligence L20:     3. cite10†2.3 The Perception-Cognition Gap L21:     4. cite11†2.4 Datasets from Social Media L22:   4. cite12†3 EmoBench-Reddit Dataset Construction L23:     1. cite13†3.1 Data Source and Filtering L24:     2. cite14†3.2 Dataset Structure and Task Design L25:       1. cite15†A. Perception Tasks L26:       2. cite16†B. Cognition Tasks L27:     3. cite17†3.3 Annotation and Quality Control L28:   5. cite18†4 Evaluation Methodology L29:     1. cite19†4.1 Tasks and Metrics L30:     2. cite20†4.2 Evaluation Strategies L31:   6. cite21†5 Experimental Setup L32:     1. cite22†5.1 Models Evaluated L33:     2. cite23†5.2 Experimental Environment L34:   7. cite24†6 Results and Analysis L35:     1. cite25†6.1 Overall Performance Overview L36:     2. cite26†6.2 Task-Level Analysis: The Perception-Cognition Gap L37:     3. cite27†6.3 Emotion-Type Analysis: Sarcasm as an Insurmountable Hurdle L38:   8. cite28†7 Discussion and Future Work L39:     1. cite29†Limitations: L40:     2. cite30†Future Work: L41:   9. cite31†8 Conclusion L42:   10. cite32†References L43: cite33†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L44: \nL45: arXiv:2509.11101v1 [cs.CL] 14 Sep 2025\nL46: # EmoBench-Reddit: A Hierarchical Benchmark for Evaluating the Emotional Intelligence of Multimodal Large Language Models\nL47: \nL48: Haokun Li Affiliation: Tianjin University    Yazhou Zhang^{*} Affiliation: Tianjin University    Jizhi Ding Affiliation: Shandong Institute of Petroleum and Chemical Technology    Qiuchi Li Affiliation: Beijing Institute of Technology    Peng Zhang^{*} Affiliation: Tianjin University\nL49: ###### Abstract\nL50: With the rapid advancement of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs), they have demonstrated exceptional capabilities across a variety of vision-language tasks. However, current evaluation benchmarks predominantly focus on objective visual question answering or captioning, inadequately assessing the models’ ability to understand complex and subjective human emotions. To bridge this gap, we introduce EmoBench-Reddit, a novel, hierarchical benchmark for multimodal emotion understanding.\nL51: The dataset comprises 350 meticulously curated samples from the social media platform Reddit, each containing an image, associated user-provided text, and an emotion category (sad, humor, sarcasm, happy) confirmed by user flairs. We designed a hierarchical task framework that progresses from basic perception to advanced cognition, with each data point featuring six multiple-choice questions and one open-ended question of increasing difficulty.\nL52: Perception tasks evaluate the model’s ability to identify basic visual elements (e.g., colors, objects), while cognition tasks require scene reasoning, intent understanding, and deep empathy integrating textual context. We ensured annotation quality through a combination of AI assistance (Claude 4) and manual verification.\nL271: Meanwhile, models like GLM-4v-plus and Qwen-vl-max demonstrate strong competitiveness on specific tasks, indicating their potential for optimization in certain capabilities. A notable example is GLM-4v-plus achieving the highest score of 0.95 on Perception 1 for \"humor,\" surpassing some first-tier models.\nL272: Models with smaller parameter counts, such as Qwen-vl-7b and Deepseek-vl-7b, struggle with complex cognitive tasks. Their scores on Cognition 2 and 3 for \"sarcasm\" are significantly lower than other models (e.g., only 0.31 on Cognition 2), clearly reflecting the importance of model capacity for understanding advanced, abstract concepts.\nL273: ### 6.2 Task-Level Analysis: The Perception-Cognition Gap\nL274: \nL275: The hierarchical design of EmoBench-Reddit allows us to clearly observe how model capabilities change across different cognitive depths.\nL276: \nL277:   * •\nL278: \nL279: Perception tasks show robust performance: Most models perform stably and well on Perception 1 (Color Recognition) and Perception 2 (Object Presence). This indicates that current leading MLLMs have strong foundational visual recognition abilities and can accurately \"see\" the content of an image.\nL280: \nL281:   * •\nL282: Cognition tasks are the main challenge: When the task shifts from \"what\" to \"why\" and \"how,\" all models exhibit a significant performance decline. Cognition 3 (Deep Reasoning) is the \"Waterloo\" for all models. For example, under the \"sad\" emotion, GPT-5’s score drops from a high of 0.97 on Perception 1 to just 0.64 on Cognition 3. This sharp drop reveals the core limitation of current models in deep emotional understanding and complex contextual reasoning.\nL283: They might recognize tears in an image but struggle to comprehend the complex social or personal reasons behind the sadness.\nL284: ### 6.3 Emotion-Type Analysis: Sarcasm as an Insurmountable Hurdle\nL285: \nL286: The intrinsic complexity of different emotions poses varying levels of challenge to the models.\nL287: \nL288:   * •\nL289: \"Sarcasm\" is the most difficult: The data clearly shows that \"sarcasm\" is the hardest emotion for all models to handle. On the Cognition 3 task, all models score very poorly; the highest score (from GPT-5) is only 0.63, while Gemini-2.0-flash plummets to 0.27. This profoundly demonstrates that understanding sarcasm is far more than recognizing the surface meaning of images and text.\nL290: It requires the ability to understand the contradiction between expectation and reality, infer the author’s true intent, and draw upon a rich repository of world knowledge and cultural context—touching the very limits of current AI’s \"Theory of Mind.\"\nL291:   * •\nL292: \nL293: \"Sad\" is the next most challenging: Cognition 3 for \"sad\" also proves difficult for most models. While GLM-4v-plus achieves a relatively high score of 0.76, most models hover around 0.6. This may be because deep understanding of sadness requires empathy—the ability to infer the potential story and emotional state behind the characters or scene, which is equally difficult for models.\nL294: \nL295:   * •\n"
